# Auto-normalize text files; Git stores LF in blobs.
* text=auto

# Force Go sources to LF in both the blob and the working tree. Without this,
# a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true produces CRLF working files, and
# `gofmt -l .` flags every .go file on CI (CI checks out LF). Pinning eol=lf
# keeps the local working tree LF too, so local `gofmt` matches CI exactly.
*.go text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for shell scripts, with a harder failure mode: these get copied to
# the Linux server and run there. CRLF makes the shebang `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`, and
# the kernel then reports "bad interpreter: No such file or directory" -- which reads
# as a missing binary rather than a line-ending problem.
#
# The blobs were already LF (text=auto normalises on commit), so a clean checkout on
# Linux was never affected. What this pins is the WORKING TREE on Windows, where
# core.autocrlf=true was producing CRLF for build.sh and verify-heatmap-server.sh.
# That matters because scripts reach the server by being copied out of a working tree
# -- /opt/docker/lodestar is not a git checkout -- so a CRLF working copy ships a
# broken script even though the repo is fine.
*.sh text eol=lf

